---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import HeroSection from "../../components/projects/the-peeking-problem/HeroSection.astro";
import LiveExperimentSection from "../../components/projects/the-peeking-problem/LiveExperimentSection.astro";
import PeekingStudySection from "../../components/projects/the-peeking-problem/PeekingStudySection.astro";
import KeyFindings from "../../components/editorial/KeyFindings.astro";
import Methodology from "../../components/editorial/Methodology.astro";
import Limitations from "../../components/editorial/Limitations.astro";
import NextSteps from "../../components/editorial/NextSteps.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
	title="The Peeking Problem"
	description="What happens when you check your A/B test every day? A live simulation of what continuous peeking costs you, in both frequentist and Bayesian terms."
>
	<HeroSection />
	<LiveExperimentSection />
	<PeekingStudySection />

	<KeyFindings
		findings={[
			{
				label: "Checking every day is almost as risky under a naive Bayesian rule as under a frequentist one",
				detail: "At default settings (10% baseline rate, 50 visitors/day per arm, 30 simulated days), checking once at a fixed end date lands close to the textbook 5% false-positive rate for both methods (4.7% frequentist, 5.3% Bayesian with a weak prior). Checking every day and stopping at the first convincing result inflates both to about 30%, roughly a 6x increase either way. A naive Bayesian probability threshold offers essentially no built-in protection against peeking on its own.",
			},
			{
				label: "A skeptical prior tempers the inflation, but it isn't free",
				detail: "Raising the Bayesian prior's strength cuts the any-day false-positive rate from 33% (weak prior) to 9% (moderate) to 5% (strong). But the same run shows the cost: the final-day-only rate drops well below 5% too, from 5.3% to 2.9% to 1.9%. A stronger prior doesn't just resist peeking, it makes the test slower to detect real effects across the board.",
			},
			{
				label: "False positives cluster early",
				detail: "Across the simulation, the median day of a first false positive is day 5 of 30 for the frequentist test (day 6 for Bayesian under a weak prior), and 71% of all frequentist false positives happen within the first third of the experiment. The moment it feels most tempting to peek, early in a test, is also the riskiest moment to trust what you see.",
			},
			{
				label: "This matches independently published numbers",
				detail: "Evan Miller's widely cited \"How Not To Run An A/B Test\" reports roughly a 26% false-positive rate when checking after every single observation at a nominal 5% threshold. This simulation's own model, built independently, lands at a comparable 29% for daily checks over 30 days. A 2025 simulation by Alex Molas found naive Bayesian posterior-threshold peeking similarly overshoots its nominal rate, consistent with the pattern shown here.",
			},
		]}
	/>

	<Methodology
		id="methodology"
		sections={[
			{
				heading: "The two engines",
				body: "Each simulated day, a pooled two-proportion z-test recomputes a two-sided p-value on the cumulative data, and a Beta-Binomial model updates each arm's posterior conversion-rate distribution, from which P(B beats A) is estimated by Monte Carlo sampling (1,500 posterior draws per day in the live panel, fewer in the repeated-trials study to stay responsive). Both engines see exactly the same accumulating data at exactly the same time.",
			},
			{
				heading: "Why a 0.975 Bayesian threshold, not 0.95",
				body: "A symmetric two-sided decision rule (declare a winner when P(B beats A) is above the threshold or below one minus it) needs its threshold calibrated before the two methods can be compared fairly. At 0.95 the Bayesian rule's single-look false-positive rate runs closer to 10%, roughly double the frequentist test's 5%, since two one-sided 95% checks compound. Calibrating to 0.975 instead brings the Bayesian single-look rate back in line with the frequentist test's, so the \"checking every day vs. checking once\" comparison on this page is apples to apples rather than comparing two differently calibrated tests.",
			},
			{
				heading: "The prior-strength slider",
				body: "Both arms start from the same Beta(k, k) prior, symmetric around 50%. Weak (k=1) is an uninformative uniform prior; strong (k=200) is a skeptical prior that takes real evidence to move away from believing the two arms are identical. This is the one dial that changes how much protection peeking gets from the Bayesian side, and it comes with a real cost (see Key Findings).",
			},
			{
				heading: "The repeated-trials peeking study",
				body: "The centerpiece comparison runs several hundred independent simulated experiments where the two arms genuinely share the same true rate (the null is true by construction), then measures how often each method would have called a winner: checking once at the end, versus checking every day and stopping at the first convincing result. This measures the classic \"optional stopping\" problem directly instead of asserting it.",
			},
			{
				heading: "What the published literature says",
				body: "Optional stopping inflating frequentist false-positive rates is a well-established result dating to Armitage, McPherson and Rowe (1969), popularized for practitioners by Evan Miller's \"How Not To Run An A/B Test\" (2010) and formalized into \"always-valid\" sequential testing by Johari, Pekelis and Walsh (2017), the basis for Optimizely's Stats Engine. Whether Bayesian methods are immune is more contested than often assumed: Rouder (2014) argued Bayes factors under certain priors resist optional stopping, but de Heide and Grünwald (2021) and Sanborn and Hills (2014) show that a naive posterior-probability threshold, exactly the kind of rule simulated here, doesn't get that protection for free, and that the severity depends heavily on the prior's informativeness, consistent with this simulation's own results. Deng, Lu and Chen (2016, Microsoft) explicitly call a fixed posterior-threshold stopping rule a \"bad practice\", distinct from a properly designed sequential Bayesian test.",
			},
		]}
	/>

	<Limitations
		items={[
			"This simulates one metric (a single conversion rate) with a stationary true rate across the whole run. Real experiments often track several metrics at once and can drift over time. Testing many metrics simultaneously compounds a different, related problem, false discovery across metrics, which isn't simulated here.",
			"The Bayesian posterior probability at each day is a Monte Carlo estimate (1,500 samples in the live panel, 250 in the repeated-trials study to stay responsive), not a closed-form number. It has its own sampling noise, smaller than the effects demonstrated here, but real; see the stats-sim test suite for a direct check of how that noise shrinks as sample count grows.",
			"Neither engine here is the properly engineered fix for peeking. Formal sequential designs (group sequential boundaries, alpha-spending functions, mSPRT-based \"always-valid\" testing) and properly constructed Bayesian stopping rules (sequential Bayes factor designs, expected-loss-based rules) are built specifically to stay valid under continuous monitoring. This project demonstrates why peeking is a problem, not how those fixes work.",
			"Some vendors describe their Bayesian A/B testing tools as immune to peeking outright. That claim can be true of an actual engineered product, if it implements a proper sequential correction internally, while being false of the naive posterior-threshold rule demonstrated on this page. Worth checking what's actually running underneath before taking a vendor's calibration claims at face value.",
		]}
	/>

	<NextSteps
		surprisingLabel="What was surprising"
		hardestLabel="Hardest part to get right"
		unresolvedLabel="What a proper fix would take"
		reflection={{
			surprising:
				"How similar the frequentist and Bayesian inflation numbers were under a weak prior, given how often \"Bayesian solves peeking\" gets repeated as received wisdom. The published literature actually settled this more carefully than the popular version suggests (see Methodology).",
			hardest:
				"Calibrating the Bayesian decision threshold to a comparable single-look false-positive rate before trusting any \"any day vs. final day\" comparison between the two methods. It would have been easy to compare an under-calibrated 0.95 threshold against a well-calibrated frequentist test and draw the wrong conclusion.",
			unresolved:
				"This project shows the problem, not the properly engineered fix. A useful follow-up would simulate an actual always-valid sequential test (mSPRT) or a formal Bayesian expected-loss stopping rule side by side with the naive rules shown here, to make the size of the fix as visible as the size of the problem.",
		}}
	/>
</BaseLayout>
